## Why This Is Asked
Low-level synchronization is core to performance. Spinlocks teach memory ordering, cache behavior, and back-off strategies that frequent high-contention code paths must consider.

## Key Concepts
- atomic_flag and memory_order semantics
- back-off strategies and CPU pause hints
- cache-line alignment and false sharing
- test-and-set versus other locking primitives

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef struct { alignas(64) atomic_flag flag; } spinlock_t;

void spinlock_init(spinlock_t *s){ atomic_flag_clear(&s->flag); }
void spin_lock(spinlock_t *s){
  int backoff = 1;
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire)) {
    for (volatile int i = 0; i < backoff; ++i) _mm_pause();
    if (backoff < 64) backoff <<= 1;
  }
}
void spin_unlock(spinlock_t *s){ atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_release); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness under high contention?
- What are trade-offs vs OS mutexes and futexes in production workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level optimization skills: cache behavior, prefetching, and reliable counting.

## Key Concepts
- Cache lines and spatial locality
- Software prefetching with __builtin_prefetch
- Loop unrolling and multiple accumulators
- Alignment and portability

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t sum_prefetch(const uint64_t *arr, size_t n){
    uint64_t a=0,b=0,c=0,d=0;
    size_t i=0;
    for(; i+3<n; i+=4){
        __builtin_prefetch(&arr[i+256],0,1);
        a += arr[i];
        b += arr[i+1];
        c += arr[i+2];
        d += arr[i+3];
    }
    for(; i<n; ++i) a += arr[i];
    return a+b+c+d;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for streaming data vs. random access?
- What changes if array length is not multiple of four?

